<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alnwick</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alnwick is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, dominated by Alnwick Castle, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-family">Percy family</a> since 1309. It lay on the invasion route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and England, and two Scottish kings met disaster there: Malcolm III was killed nearby in 1093, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a> was captured outside the town in 1174, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-falaise">Treaty of Falaise</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alnwick.jpg" alt="Alnwick" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alnwick_-_Alnwick_Castle_-_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alnwick is the traditional county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, standing on the south bank of the River Aln, 32 miles south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a> and the Scottish border, 5 miles inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at Alnmouth and 34 miles north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>. Its name comes from the Old English wic, meaning dairy farm or settlement, and the name of the river. The town dates to about 600 CE and thrived as an agricultural centre. It was a staging post on the Great North Road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and its history is largely the history of the castle and its lords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>'s standard-bearer Gilbert Tyson received the estate. It then passed to the De Vesci family, who held it for over 200 years, and in 1309 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-family">House of Percy</a>. Ivo de Vesci founded the castle as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> structure about 1096, and David I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> captured it in 1136, the first recorded capture. Alnwick Abbey, for Premonstratensian canons, was founded in 1147, and about then the castle was rebuilt in stone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-iii-of-scotland">Malcolm III of Scotland</a> was killed in the first Battle of Alnwick in 1093, and in 1174 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a> was captured by about 400 mounted knights led by Ranulf de Glanvill.</p><p>Scottish raiders burnt much of the town in 1424 and again in 1448, after which the town was walled. Henry IV took the castle in 1403 after the Percys challenged him. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> it changed hands repeatedly, and finally fell to the Yorkists on 23 June 1463, which made it, according to the historian Dan Spencer, the most besieged place in the country in that war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Both Alnwick Abbey and Hulne Priory, a Carmelite friary founded about 1240, were suppressed in 1539, and the Percys left the castle to decay until they resumed residence in the mid-18th century, since when they have remained there. The castle stands in grounds landscaped by Capability Brown and is one of the homes of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>. The Bondgate Tower is the only upstanding part of the medieval town wall. St Michael's Church on Bailiffgate dates from the 15th century with fragments of the 12th. Brizlee Tower, designed by Robert Adam in 1777 and erected in 1781, stands in Hulne Park. The population at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> was 8,430.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alnwick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English knights surprised King William I of Scotland in fog outside <b>Alnwick</b> and took him prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Malcolm III was killed in an ambush while besieging <b>Alnwick</b> Castle in Northumberland, together with his eldest son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Margaret, wife of Malcolm III, died in Edinburgh Castle days after learning that her husband and eldest son had been killed at <b>Alnwick</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
